{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775379068-x1o2iDVVZ3oKtbRzMbcp","role":"assistant","created":1775379073,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Korean War started in June 1950. Let me confirm this historically. The Korean War began on June 25, 1950, when North Korean forces crossed the 38th parallel and invaded South Korea.\n\nFor fiscal years in the US:\n- FY 1950 would be from July 1, 1949 to June 30, 1950\n- FY 1951 would be from July 1, 1950 to June 30, 1951\n\nSince the Korean War started in June 1950, it would be during FY 1950 (which ends June 30, 1950). Actually wait, let me think about this more carefully.\n\nThe Korean War started on June 25, 1950. The US fiscal year ends on June 30. So the Korean War started very late in FY 1950 (almost at the very end). However, the question asks about \"the fiscal year during which the Korean War started\" - this could be interpreted as FY 1950.\n\nBut wait - since the Korean War started on June 25, 1950 (near the end of FY 1950), and we're looking at data from this period, I need to determine what value to use.\n\nLooking at the data from the page 25 text I saw:\n- 1947: Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund = .4 (million dollars)\n\nActually wait - looking more carefully at the table structure. Let me look at the column headers again:\n\nThere's a table with:\n- First table shows \"Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund 4/\" which shows amounts like 1,459.5 for 1947\n- Second table shows \"Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund\" which shows .4 for 1947\n\nThe question asks about \"expenditure transfers to the trust fund for Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance\". I think this is the second table's \"Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund\" column.\n\n\nI'll investigate the 1950 data to understand the transfers during the Korean War's start. The data shows transfers of 3.6 million dollars for that year, providing a key data point for my analysis.\n","signature":""},{"