{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775379096-XBzsFLvWkRvCGKP8FTK4","role":"assistant","created":1775379100,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, let me reconsider. The question asks about \"expenditure transfers to the trust fund for Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance\".\n\nLooking at the table, there's a column labeled \"Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund\" (F.O.A. and S.I. = Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance).\n\nBut wait - there are also two tables:\n1. First table shows \"Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund\" as deductions from receipts\n2. Second table shows \"Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund\" as expenditure\n\nLet me re-read the question: \"expenditure transfers to the trust fund for Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance\"\n\nThis sounds like it's asking about the expenditures/transfers shown in the second table (the expenditure side).\n\nFrom the second table:\n- 1947: .4 (million)\n- 1950: 3.6 (million)\n\nThe calculation I did is correct. But let me double-check:\n- Korean War started June 25, 1950\n- FY 1950 runs July 1, 1949 to June 30, 1950\n- The Korean War started near the very end of FY 1950, so FY 1950 is indeed the fiscal year during which the Korean War started.\n\nCAGR = (3.6/0.4)^(1/3) - 1 = 9^(1/3) - 1 ≈ 2.0801 - 1 = 1.0801 = 108.01%\n\nThe answer is 108.01% per year.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_db5633ccff1145c29502d282","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/app/answer.txt","content":"108.01"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
